<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="fo7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>FO7: Equilibrium Points &amp; Autonomous Equations</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can determine the equilibrium solutions of autonomous differential equations and use a phase line (and associated terminology) to describe their behavior.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-autonomous-equations">
        <title>Autonomous Equations</title>
        <definition xml:id="def-autonomous-equation">
            <title>Autonomous Equation</title>
            <idx><h>autonomous equation</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A differential equation is said to be <term>autonomous</term> if it can be written in the form 
                    <md>
                        y' = f(y)
                    </md>
                    That is, the differential equation does not depend on the independent variable.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Solving Autonomous Equations</title>
            <p>
                All autonomous equations are separable, and so can be solved using that technique; not all will result in closed-form solutions.
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example xml:id="fo7-autonomous-ex-logistic">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Autonomous equations have particularly nice slope fields. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    Consider the differential equation 
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 0.1x(2-x)
                    </md>
                    This is of the form given by <xref ref="logistic-growth"/>, so we should expect solutions to look like logistic functions. Indeed, solving results in 
                    <md>
                        y(x) = \dfrac{5}{1+Ce^{0.5x}}
                    </md>
                    for some constant <m>C</m>. The resulting slope field takes the form below.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fo7-slopefield-1">
                    <image width="60%">
                        <xi:include href="img/fo7-slopefield-1.xml"/>
                    </image>
                    <caption>The slope field for the equation <m>y' = 0.1y(2-y)</m>.</caption>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The blue solution curves correspond to initial values for which the solution is bounded below by 0 and grows to the carrying capacity of the system; the red solution curves show initial values below zero, and the green solution curves show initial values above <m>y(x) = 5</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Note the horizontal lines which create asymptotes for the solution curves. We call these output values <em>equilibrium solutions</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-equilibrium-solutions-autonomous">
        <title>Equilibrium Solutions &amp; Phase Lines</title>
        
        
        <definition xml:id="def-equilibrium-solution">
            <title>Equilibrium Solution</title>
            <idx><h>equilibrium solution (autonomous equation)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An <term>equilibrium solution</term> for an autonomous ODE <m>y' = f(y)</m> occurs when
                    <md>
                        f(y) = 0
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In <xref ref="fo7-autonomous-ex-logistic"/>, we see from <xref ref="fo7-slopefield-1"/> that there are two equilibrium solutions: <m>y(x) = 5</m> and <m>y(x) = 0</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    If we assume that the example describes the growth of a population, then the equilibrium solutions are communicating the carrying capacity of the population (<m>y(x) = 5</m>) and the zero-population point (<m>y(x) = 0</m>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    For initial values within the interval <m>[0,5]</m>, we see population growth to the equilibrium point; for initial values outside of the equilibrium solutions, we see either a tendency away from (initial conditions below 0) or toward (initial conditions above 5) the equilibrium solutions. Physically, the first set of initial conditions don't make sense in a population context, but the second set of initial conditions demonstrate that when population is above the carrying capacity, it will always tend back toward the carrying capacity of the system.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
        
        <note>
            <p>
                When analyzing the qualitative behavior of autonomous differential equations, we can use a <em>phase line</em> or <em>phase diagram</em> to capture the entirety of the behavior without sketching the slope field.
            </p>
        </note>

        <definition xml:id="def-phase-line">
            <title>Phase Line/Phase Diagram</title>
            <idx><h>phase line</h></idx>
            <idx><h>phase diagram</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For an autonomous differential equation, the <term>phase line</term> or <term>phase diagram</term> for the differential equation is a diagram which captures the long-term behavior, drawn in the form of a line.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Again drawing on <xref ref="fo7-autonomous-ex-logistic"/>, from the slope field in <xref ref="fo7-slopefield-1"/>, we note that initial conditions above the carrying capacity result in solutions tending down toward the carrying capacity, while those below the zero-point tend toward <m>-\infty</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    To draw the phase line, draw a line (traditionally vertical, but horizontal communicates the same idea) with the equilibrium points marked.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then, use the information given to draw arrows in the segments created to indicate the direction of flow of the solutions. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    The phase line for <xref ref="fo7-autonomous-ex-logistic"/> is shown below.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fo7-phase-line">
                    <image width ="60%">
                        <xi:include href="img/fo7-phaseline-1.xml"/>
                    </image>
                    <caption>The phase line for the solutions to the equation <m>y' = 0.1y(5-y)</m></caption>
                </figure>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <definition xml:id="def-equilibrium-source-sink-node">
            <title>Source/Sink/Node</title>
            <idx><h>source</h></idx>
            <idx><h>sink</h></idx>
            <idx><h>node</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An equilibrium solution is said to be a <term>source</term> if solution curves nearby tend away from the equilibrium solution.
                </p>
                <p>
                    An equilibrium solution is said to be a <term>sink</term> if solution curves nearby tend toward the equilibrium solution.
                </p>
                <p>
                    An equilibrium solution is said to be a <term>node</term> if it exhibits both source and sink behavior.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For <xref ref="fo7-autonomous-ex-logistic"/> and <xref ref="fo7-phase-line"/>, the equilibrium solution <m>y(x) = 5</m> is a sink because the solutions on either side tend toward 5; on the other hand, <m>y(x) = 0</m> is a source because the solutions on either side tend away from 0.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each differential equation below, draw the phase line and classify each equilibrium solution as a sink, source, or node.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>y' = y(y-2)(y+3)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>y' = y^2(y-2)(y+1)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>y' = \cos(y)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>y' = \sin^2(y)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>

</section>